      "id": 3,
      "claim": "High concentrations of chlorophyll produce uniformly deep green leaves that mask other pigments.",
      "evidence": "Plant pigment biology shows chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light, reflecting green; when abundant it overwhelms carotenoids and anthocyanins, yielding a solid green appearance.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "For variegated or colored hostas, the aesthetic optimum is the visibility of their non‑green features; a leaf that is all green would not be considered looking its best.",
      "evidence": "Gardening guides warn that excessive chlorophyll (e.g., from too much sun) causes variegated hostas to \"green out,\" reducing their ornamental value.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, hostas do not need lots of chlorophyll to look their best; in many prized varieties, reduced chlorophyll is essential to the desired appearance.",
      "evidence": "Combining steps 2‑4 shows that excessive chlorophyll would diminish the ornamental traits that define the plant’s visual appeal, while sufficient (but not excessive) chlorophyll is enough for basic health.",
